---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.4 — Nested Loops: The GridArchitect &amp; Fog-of-War Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topic 2.11 · 90-Minute Design-First Build.</strong> Whenever a problem has two
    dimensions—rows and columns on a dungeon map, or comparing every item against every other item—you
    place one loop inside another. For every <em>single</em> step of the outer loop, the inner loop
    runs from start to finish.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (One-Shot Challenge):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/GridArchitect.java" download>GridArchitect.java</a> — starter file with
            Javadocs, method stubs, and a built-in <strong>Dungeon Map Renderer Preview</strong> in
            <code>main()</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Draw tiny grids (<code>2&times;2</code>,
            <code>3&times;4</code>, <code>height = 4</code>) on scratch paper and answer the
            Guided Design questions for loop bounds, local variables, branch order, and newline
            placement.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; render in the sandbox:</strong> Draft your method code in the box,
            click <strong>Copy</strong> to paste it into <code>GridArchitect.java</code>, and
            inspect your rendered ASCII maps with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac GridArchitect.java
java GridArchitect</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (One-Shot Pass):</strong> Once every map and pair count in your sandbox looks
        exact, unlock <code>GridArchitectCheck.java</code> below and aim to pass all
        <strong>13 checks</strong> on your first try.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Rectangular Grid Coordinates</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>buildBorderedRoom(int rows, int cols)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Before a dungeon level can spawn monsters or loot, the map generator constructs an ASCII
            chamber framed by solid stone walls (<code>"#"</code>) around an open floor
            (<code>"."</code>). Returning the whole room as a single multi-line <code>String</code>
            lets the renderer print it, save it to a file, or compare maps in tests.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int rows</code> (precondition: <code>rows &gt;= 2</code>) — total row count,
                indexed <code>0 .. rows - 1</code>.</li>
            <li><code>int cols</code> (precondition: <code>cols &gt;= 2</code>) — total column count,
                indexed <code>0 .. cols - 1</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>String</code>):</strong> a multi-line string of
                <code>rows</code> lines, each containing <code>cols</code> characters
                (<code>"#"</code> on the outer edge, <code>"."</code> inside) followed by
                <code>"\\n"</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>buildBorderedRoom(3, 4)</code> &rarr;
                <code>"####\\n#..#\\n####\\n"</code> (rows <code>0</code> and <code>2</code> and
                columns <code>0</code> and <code>3</code> are walls; only <code>(1,1)</code> and
                <code>(1,2)</code> are floor).</li>
            <li><code>buildBorderedRoom(2, 2)</code> &rarr; <code>"##\\n##\\n"</code> (zero
                interior cells because every cell touches an edge).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>buildBorderedRoom</code></h3>
        <p>Answer these design questions before writing your nested loops:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Outer vs. Inner Loop &amp; Newline Placement:</strong> Why must the outer
                <code>for</code> loop iterate over rows <code>r</code> and the inner <code>for</code>
                loop iterate over columns <code>c</code> (and not the other way around)? Where must
                <code>result += "\\n";</code> execute relative to the inner loop braces so you get
                one line per row instead of one character per line?</li>
            <li><strong>Border Condition:</strong> In terms of <code>r</code>, <code>c</code>,
                <code>rows</code>, and <code>cols</code>, what compound boolean condition identifies
                every outer-border cell that should receive <code>"#"</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>GridArchitect.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>buildBorderedRoom</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>GridArchitect.java</code>, and run <code>java GridArchitect</code> to inspect
                the <code>3x4</code> and <code>2x2</code> chambers?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Distance Math &amp; Branch Priority</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>renderFogOfWar(int size, int heroRow, int heroCol, int visionRadius)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            In a stealth dungeon crawl, the hero carries a lantern whose light fades after
            <code>visionRadius</code> orthogonal steps. Using <strong>Manhattan distance</strong>
            (<code>Math.abs(r - heroRow) + Math.abs(c - heroCol)</code>, the number of up/down/left/right
            grid moves between two tiles), <code>renderFogOfWar</code> draws a radar map showing
            where the hero stands (<code>"@"</code>), what tiles are lit (<code>"."</code>), and
            what stays hidden in darkness (<code>"?"</code>).
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int size</code> — width and height of the square grid (<code>0 .. size - 1</code>).</li>
            <li><code>int heroRow</code>, <code>int heroCol</code> — the hero's 0-based grid coordinates.</li>
            <li><code>int visionRadius</code> — maximum Manhattan distance lit by the hero's lantern.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>String</code>):</strong> <code>size</code> rows of
                <code>"@"</code> / <code>"."</code> / <code>"?"</code>, each ending with
                <code>"\\n"</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>renderFogOfWar(5, 2, 2, 1)</code> &rarr; center <code>(2,2)</code> is
                <code>@</code>; the 4 compass neighbors at distance <code>1</code> are
                <code>.</code>; diagonal corners like <code>(1,1)</code> have distance
                <code>1 + 1 = 2 &gt; 1</code> so they are <code>?</code>, forming a diamond!</li>
            <li><code>renderFogOfWar(3, 1, 1, 0)</code> &rarr; <code>"???\\n?@?\\n???\\n"</code>
                (even with radius <code>0</code>, the hero's own tile must display <code>@</code>).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>renderFogOfWar</code></h3>
        <p>Plan the calculation and branch ordering before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Local Variable &amp; Geometry:</strong> Inside the inner column loop, what
                local variable should you compute using <code>Math.abs(...)</code>? Why does
                <code>dist &lt;= 1</code> light a 5-tile diamond rather than a 3&times;3 square?</li>
            <li><strong>Order of Branches (Edge Case):</strong> At the hero's own position
                <code>(heroRow, heroCol)</code>, what does Manhattan distance evaluate to? Why must
                the <code>if</code> check for the hero tile (<code>"@"</code>) come <em>before</em>
                checking <code>dist &lt;= visionRadius</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>GridArchitect.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>renderFogOfWar</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>GridArchitect.java</code>, and inspect the <code>5x5</code> diamond and
                <code>3x3</code> radius-<code>0</code> maps in <code>java GridArchitect</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Sequential Inner Loops</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>buildRightStaircase(int height)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            To climb between dungeon levels, the map builder renders a right-aligned stone staircase
            where empty air on the left (<code>"."</code>) shrinks as solid stone steps on the right
            (<code>"#"</code>) grow wider each row. Unlike a uniform grid, each row requires two
            different counts that depend on the current row number.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int height</code> — total number of steps (rows <code>1 .. height</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>String</code>):</strong> a string where row <code>r</code> has
                <code>height - r</code> dots followed by <code>r</code> hashes and <code>"\\n"</code>,
                or <code>""</code> if <code>height &lt;= 0</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>buildRightStaircase(4)</code> &rarr; row 1: <code>...#</code>, row 2:
                <code>..##</code>, row 3: <code>.###</code>, row 4: <code>####</code>.</li>
            <li><code>buildRightStaircase(1)</code> &rarr; <code>"#\\n"</code> (0 dots, 1 hash).</li>
            <li><code>buildRightStaircase(0)</code> &rarr; <code>""</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>buildRightStaircase</code></h3>
        <p>Map out the outer and inner loop structure:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Sequential vs. Nested Inner Loops:</strong> Inside the outer row loop
                (<code>r = 1 .. height</code>), why do we need two <em>consecutive</em> inner loops
                (one after the other) rather than nesting the hash loop inside the dot loop? In
                terms of <code>height</code> and <code>r</code>, how many times does each inner loop
                run on row <code>r</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Boundary Input:</strong> If your outer loop runs from
                <code>int r = 1; r &lt;= height; r++</code> and <code>result</code> starts as
                <code>""</code>, what happens automatically when <code>height &lt;= 0</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>GridArchitect.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>buildRightStaircase</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>GridArchitect.java</code>, and verify <code>height = 4</code> and
                <code>height = 0</code> in <code>java GridArchitect</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · Dependent Inner Bounds &amp; Unique Pairs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>countLuckyLootPairs(int maxTier)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            A master blacksmith can fuse two treasure chests of <strong>distinct</strong> tiers
            <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> (each between <code>1</code> and <code>maxTier</code>)
            into a Lucky Relic whenever their combined tier <code>a + b</code> is divisible by
            <code>3</code>. Order does not matter: fusing tiers <code>(1, 2)</code> is the exact
            same combination as <code>(2, 1)</code>, and a tier cannot fuse with itself. Counting
            unique unordered pairs is a classic AP CS A nested-loop pattern that reappears in Unit 6
            and Unit 7 when checking arrays/lists for duplicate or matching pairs!
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int maxTier</code> — highest integer chest tier available (tiers are
                <code>1 .. maxTier</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>int</code>):</strong> the number of distinct unordered pairs
                <code>{a, b}</code> with <code>1 &lt;= a &lt; b &lt;= maxTier</code> where
                <code>(a + b) % 3 == 0</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>maxTier = 1</code> &rarr; <code>0</code> (only tier <code>1</code> exists; no
                second distinct tier to pair with).</li>
            <li><code>maxTier = 4</code> &rarr; <code>2</code> (the valid unique pairs among
                <code>1..4</code> are <code>(1,2)</code> and <code>(2,4)</code> — <em>not</em>
                <code>(2,1)</code>, <code>(4,2)</code>, or <code>(3,3)</code>!).</li>
            <li><code>maxTier = 6</code> &rarr; <code>5</code> (<code>(1,2), (1,5), (2,4), (3,6), (4,5)</code>).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>countLuckyLootPairs</code></h3>
        <p>Think through how loop bounds prevent double-counting:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Why Naive Loops Fail:</strong> If both the outer loop (<code>a</code>) and
                inner loop (<code>b</code>) start at <code>1</code> and go to <code>maxTier</code>,
                what two kinds of invalid pairs get counted when <code>maxTier = 4</code> (making
                it return <code>5</code> instead of <code>2</code>)?</li>
            <li><strong>Dependent Inner Bound:</strong> How should you initialize the inner loop
                variable <code>b</code> in terms of <code>a</code> so that <code>a &lt; b</code> is
                always guaranteed, preventing both self-pairs and reversed duplicates while also
                returning <code>0</code> cleanly when <code>maxTier &lt;= 1</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>GridArchitect.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>countLuckyLootPairs</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>GridArchitect.java</code>, and confirm <code>java GridArchitect</code> prints
                <code>0, 1, 2, 5</code> for tiers <code>1, 2, 4, 6</code> before unlocking
                <code>GridArchitectCheck.java</code> below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods render cleanly in <code>java GridArchitect</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>GridArchitectCheck.java</code> below and aim for <strong><code>13 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> on your first run</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/nested-loops.html">Nested Loops</a> — outer/inner mechanics, dependent bounds</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/math-class.html">Math Class</a> — Math.abs() for Manhattan distance</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
